0014: a mutable image without a format list keeps the tiling its class allows.

Why. A VkImageCreateInfo with VK_IMAGE_CREATE_MUTABLE_FORMAT_BIT and no
VkImageFormatListCreateInfo may be viewed as any format compatible with its
base format, but the driver does not know which, so the three predicates in
tu6_mutable_format_list_forces_linear() are handed a NULL and answer the
worst case:

  tu6_format_list_has_swaps(NULL)                = true
  tu6_format_list_reinterprets_r8g8_r16(NULL)    = true
  tu6_format_list_swaps_are_uniform(NULL)        = false

i.e. "there is a swap, there is an R8G8/R16 reinterpretation, and the swaps
are not uniform" - the image goes linear even when every format the app can
legally view it as has the same layout. This is not a corner case: in the
RE4 inventory (results/raw/game-patched-imginv4.log) 4062 MiB of 7977 MiB
sampled, 50.9%, are MUTABLE images with listn=0, and all of them are in
linear.

The class. tu6_mutable_compat_class() builds the set of formats the base
format may be viewed as, and the existing predicates then run on that set
instead of on NULL. Two properties make this safe without a copy of the
Vulkan compatibility table:

  - the grouping is the texel block: same bits per block, same block width
    and height, same compressed-ness. The tiled layout is a function of the
    block size alone (fd6_layout.c: layout->cpp =
    util_format_get_blocksize(), and the tile height follows cpp), so a
    format outside the block class can never describe the same memory. It
    is therefore a superset of the spec class ("occupies the same number of
    bits per texel block"), and a superset can only make the answer more
    conservative;
  - what is left inside the block class is the channel order, and that is
    exactly what tu6_format_list_has_swaps() and
    tu6_format_list_swaps_are_uniform() already compare. So the same
    reasoning that decides an explicit list decides the class.

Only formats the driver can actually use are put into the class: a view is
either sampled or written, and block-compressed formats are texture-only in
fd6_format_table.c (_T_ leaves rb = NONE), so the filter has to consult
fd6_color_format() and fd6_texture_format() together. With
fd6_color_format() alone every BC format would drop out of its own class and
BC would lose the tiling for no reason (measured: BC7 stayed linear until
the texture side was added).

What the class answer is, per block class. Measured with probe NL in
tests/rp6-vkd3d-sparse-test.c, 500x500 (not a multiple of the tile, so the
two layouts have different totals), results/test-0014-ship.log (sha256
c17d02bcaae9, no instrumentation in the tree; the same matrix on the
instrumented build 050375a18b7b is results/test-0014-final.log; the
intermediate build 97072a8f6376, before the UBWC-gate split below, gave the
same 94/94):

  class                        base format            layout   why
  8-bit (1 B/texel)            R8_UNORM               tiled    no member needs a swap
  8-byte (64 bit)              R16G16B16A16_FLOAT,    tiled    every member is WZYX
                               R32G32_FLOAT
  block-compressed             BC7_UNORM              tiled    every member is WZYX
  4-byte (32 bit)              R8G8B8A8_UNORM,        linear   the class holds both
                               R32_UINT                        RGBA8 and BGRA8 orders
  2-byte (16 bit)              R8G8_UNORM, R16_UNORM  linear   the class holds R8G8
                                                               and R16

The last two rows are not a limitation to be fixed later: they are the same
demotion that 0005/0009/0010 guard on explicit lists, and the cross-order
read through such a class is genuinely undefined by the layout. The gain is
in the first three: from the same inventory, 2201 MiB of the 3801 MiB
deposed (57.9%) go back to tiled - 1171 MiB of 8-bit, 585 MiB of 64-bit,
445 MiB of BC; without the BC classes, which need the texture-side filter
above, 1756 MiB.

How the layout is detected. memreq separates the two layouts only when the
image is not a multiple of the tile: tiled pads to the tile, linear pads
rows. For block-compressed formats at 500x500 the two totals are equal
(0x40000 both), so the authoritative detector is the sparse create: a
linear image cannot be sparse (tu_image_init() refuses it, see 0004), so
SUCCESS means tiled and FEATURE_NOT_PRESENT means linear. Both detectors
agree with the class model on all 8 probes, and all 8 fill+readback
roundtrips are byte-exact (0 bytes differ) - the tiled layout the class
allows is one the r2d paths can address. The roundtrip needs 0013: the fill
is a buffer->image copy, whose writes are not visible to the reader without
the CCU clean.

The format query has to agree. tu_get_image_format_properties() refuses
sparse+mutable when tu6_mutable_format_list_forces_linear() says the image
would be linear, and it sees the same NULL. It now synthesizes the same
class, so a sparse+mutable image without a list is advertised and created
for the 8-bit and 64-bit classes and refused for the 4-byte and 2-byte ones
- the probe's sparse-create column is that agreement, observed from the
other side.

UBWC is deliberately not touched.  tu6_mutable_format_list_ubwc_compatible()
and format_list_ubwc_possible() are still handed the original list pointer
(ubwc_list), which is NULL when the app passed none, so a no-list mutable
image still loses UBWC exactly as in stock.  Only the tiling predicates are
answered with the class.  Keeping compression on such an image would need its
own content check (UBWC metadata reinterpretation is the thing 0011/0012 and
exp-1/exp-2 were about), and mixing the two decisions in one patch would make
the byte result above unattributable.

What stays out / residual risk. The content check is a fill and readback in
the base format; a view through a *different* member of the same class
(R32_FLOAT over R8G8B8A8, say) is not exercised here, and the justification
for it is the layout argument, not a measurement. For the classes this
patch actually moves to tiled that argument is the same one 0005/0006 use
for explicit lists, and the class members there are all WZYX with the same
block, so there is nothing to disagree about; but the honest statement is
that the tiled-no-list cross-view read has not been run.

Relations: standalone in mechanism, depends on 0004 only because
tu6_mutable_format_list_forces_linear() is introduced there. Complements
0005/0009/0010 (they decide explicit lists, this decides the absent one)
and 0013 (needed for the roundtrip). No effect on explicit lists: the class
is only built when the app passed none.

